          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What TSH range does guidance describe as the treatment threshold in subclinical hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe retrieved guidance gives one clear number. In the hypothyroidism guidance (Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change\"), there is broad agreement on treating when thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is persistently above about 10 mIU/L.\n\nThe same document says the band between the upper reference limit and 10 mIU/L is disputed. Some guidance suggests a trial of treatment when symptoms, positive antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present. Other guidance suggests monitoring alone for most people. Pregnancy is handled separately, with a lower threshold for treatment.\n\nThe passages I received do not give the exact upper reference limit, and they do not explain how the guidance defines \"persistently.\" A clinician can help interpret a specific result.",
